course: dictionary
lesson: 2040
title: Congratulate: saying well done
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To congratulate someone means to tell them you are pleased about their success, good luck or a happy event. «We congratulated her on winning the race.» You say it to show you are happy for them. It is a verb, so it changes: congratulate, congratulates, congratulated, congratulating.
explain: The word has a second meaning too. «She congratulated herself on finishing the puzzle.» Nobody else is needed here. She felt pleased with herself for something she did.
explain: Look at the words around it to work out which meaning is used. «A good loser congratulates the winner» tells you that even someone who lost can be kind and say well done. Congratulate is a friendly action, not a rude one.
fact: Congratulate means to tell someone you are pleased about their success, good luck or happy news.
fact: To congratulate yourself means to feel pleased with yourself for something you did.
fact: Congratulate is a verb, so it changes: congratulates, congratulated, congratulating.
fact: «Congratulations!» is what people say on birthdays, new babies, weddings and prizes.
fact: After a game, a good winner says «good game», and a good loser congratulates the winner.
exercise: «We congratulated her on winning the race.» What does congratulated mean here? | *Told her we were pleased about her win | Shouted at her for being too slow | Ran away from her in fright | Asked her to race us again
exercise: «A good loser congratulates the winner.» What does a good loser do? | *Says well done and is kind about the win | Asks for the game to be cancelled | Hides the prize | Ignores the winner and sulks
exercise: «She congratulated herself on finishing the puzzle.» Find and copy the word that shows what she did. | she | *congratulated | puzzle | finishing
test: «Yesterday we ___ our friend on her birthday.» Which word fits? | congratulating | *congratulated | congratulates | congratulate
test: Nadia says congratulate means to be cross with someone. What does it really mean? | *To tell someone you are pleased about their success | To race someone to the finish | To tell someone off for losing | To hide a prize from someone
exam: «After the party game, Sam shook hands, congratulated the winner and smiled.» What does this show about Sam? | Sam wanted to leave the party early | Sam was cheating in the game | *Good sportsmanship, being kind even when he lost | Sam was angry about the result
